{"Irish":{"id":"feb868c7-d2c6-4081-967f-24cafee8d634","word":"Irish","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"pertaining to or originating from Ireland or the Irish people","word":"irlandês"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"pertaining to the language","word":"irlandês"}],"pos":"adj","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Sheep are typical in the Irish landscape.","bold_text_offsets":[[25,30]],"type":"example","id":"c0709d56-d448-4041-84f4-bee310b31e47"},{"text":"A. Fink-Nottle: But it's absolute balderdash, Bertie. I mean, listen to this: \"Sure and begorrah, I don't know what's after being the matter with you, Michael.\" I mean, what on earth is this \"what's after being\" stuff mean?\nB.W. Wooster: My dear old Gussie, that is how people think Irish people talk.","ref":"1992 April 26, “Hot Off the Press”, in Jeeves and Wooster, Series 3, Episode 5:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[283,288]],"id":"2a870af1-7419-46ec-8bcf-59eec0f9c669"},{"text":"My genes would best be described as a melting pot – my mother is part Bulgarian Roma, part Romanichal (English Romany), and my dad is part Romanichal, part Irish Traveller – thus, it was hardly surprising when I was born a blue-eyed milk bottle.","ref":"2013 October 25, Filip Borev, “I was a pale Roma baby – it's always been a family joke that I was stolen”, in The Guardian, →ISSN, archived from the original on 12 Aug 2022:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[156,161]],"id":"43aff466-7c99-4f55-a764-ee1d414fc6ba"}],"links":[["Ireland","Ireland"],["Irish","Irish"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples","Quotation templates to be cleaned"],"glosses":["Pertaining to or originating from Ireland or the Irish people."],"id":"c21ee4b9-2da9-454e-9535-36c32b2c3c0e"},{"links":[["language","language"]],"glosses":["Pertaining to the Irish language."],"id":"17fb5d56-f976-441c-b310-cbb62521ec74"}],"related":[{"word":"Erse"},{"word":"Gaelic"},{"word":"Wiktionary’s coverage of Irish terms"}],"synonyms":[]},"iridium":{"id":"5408ea0b-4295-491b-b1fc-f73a8501024b","word":"iridium","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"chemical element","tags":["masculine"],"word":"irídio"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Iridium is extremely rare on the surface of the earth but much more common in meteorites.","ref":"2014, Elizabeth Kolbert, The Sixth Extinction: An Unnatural History, Picador, →ISBN, page 75:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[0,7]],"id":"53abc035-c729-4fd6-80c5-97878fef60db"}],"wikidata":["Q877"],"senseid":["en:Q877"],"categories":["English terms with quotations","English uncountable nouns"],"links":[["atomic number","atomic number"],["transition metal","transition metal"],["platinum group","platinum group"]],"related":[{"word":"mul:Ir","tags":["alternative"]}],"raw_glosses":["(uncountable) A chemical element with an atomic number of 77: a very hard, brittle, silvery-white transition metal of the platinum group."],"glosses":["A chemical element with an atomic number of 77: a very hard, brittle, silvery-white transition metal of the platinum group."],"tags":["uncountable"],"id":"6d9fdab6-0c5e-42f8-b3c8-76c00cd3fe4c"},{"categories":["English countable nouns"],"raw_glosses":["(countable) A single atom of this element."],"glosses":["A single atom of this element."],"tags":["countable"],"id":"ebbc6c22-8f63-4e8b-a0ea-0ccd8b17a82a"}],"related":[{"word":"iridescence"},{"word":"iridin"},{"word":"iridiosmium"},{"word":"iridise"},{"word":"iridize"},{"word":"iris"}],"synonyms":[]},"iris":{"id":"d4c74491-185f-4e69-ba3a-a8c3f0871d95","word":"iris","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"plant of the genus Iris","tags":["masculine"],"word":"íris"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"part of the eye","tags":["masculine"],"word":"íris"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Breezes blowing from beds of iris quickened her breath with their perfume; she saw the tufted lilacs sway in the wind, and the streamers of mauve-tinted wistaria swinging, all a-glisten with golden bees; she saw a crimson cardinal winging through the foliage, and amorous tanagers flashing like scarlet flames athwart the pines.","ref":"1907 August, Robert W[illiam] Chambers, “Afterglow”, in The Younger Set, New York, N.Y.: D. Appleton & Company, →OCLC, page 168:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[29,33]],"id":"24b9b05d-48a0-4813-a3ad-48c4c100a303"}],"topics":["biology","botany","natural-sciences"],"wikidata":["Q156901"],"senseid":["en:Q156901"],"categories":["English terms with quotations","en:Botany"],"links":[["botany","botany"],["plant","plant"],["Iris","Iris"],["Iris (plant)","w:Iris (plant)"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"fleur-de-lis"}],"raw_glosses":["(botany) A plant of the genus Iris, common in the northern hemisphere, and generally having attractive blooms (See Iris (plant) on Wikipedia.Wikipedia )."],"glosses":["A plant of the genus Iris, common in the northern hemisphere, and generally having attractive blooms (See Iris (plant) on Wikipedia.Wikipedia )."],"id":"8097f445-c18e-439f-804f-5a09bd928d0e"},{"topics":["anatomy","medicine","sciences"],"wikidata":["Q178748"],"senseid":["en:Q178748"],"categories":["en:Anatomy"],"links":[["anatomy","anatomy"],["contractile","contractile"],["membrane","membrane"],["perforate","perforate"],["pupil","pupil"],["retina","retina"],["eye","eye"],["Iris (anatomy)","w:Iris (anatomy)"]],"raw_glosses":["(anatomy) The contractile membrane perforated by the pupil, which adjusts to control the amount of light reaching the retina, and which forms the colored portion of the eye (See Iris (anatomy) on Wikipedia.Wikipedia )."],"glosses":["The contractile membrane perforated by the pupil, which adjusts to control the amount of light reaching the retina, and which forms the colored portion of the eye (See Iris (anatomy) on Wikipedia.Wikip